<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Price Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th–17th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Price Revolution was a period of sustained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> during the sixteenth and early seventeenth centuries. It was driven in part by the influx of American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> flowing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to the bankers who financed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> campaigns. The inflation damaged Spanish agriculture and manufacturing, weakening the economic base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Prices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> rose roughly sixfold over 150 years, which amounts to about 1.2 per cent a year compounded. That is low by modern standards but high for the monetary system of the 16th century, which rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> of about 1520 to 1640 is usually linked to the large influx of gold and silver from the Spanish treasure fleet. Starting in the 1540s, silver came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a> and Taxco in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and the mountain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and Potosí's output rose in the 1560s after mercury deposits were found in the Andes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Specie entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> raised its prices and caused a balance of payments deficit, which spread the metal to the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. Earl J. Hamilton counted imports of around 210 million pesos in the 16th century, 160 million of them after 1550. Martín de Azpilcueta linked American silver to rising prices in 1556, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-bodin">Jean Bodin</a> made the case in 1568 against Jean de Malestroit, who blamed debased coin. Spain debased little until 1599, when Philip III introduced a purely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> vellon coinage.</p><p>Other causes are proposed. Population growth after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> raised demand for food, and grain prices rose sooner and faster than others. Urbanisation and the enclosure of common land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> increased the pace of trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> output in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> also rose to a peak in the 1530s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> borrowed heavily from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugger-family">Fuggers</a>, the Welsers and Genoese banking families to be elected emperor and to fund his policy, repaying them from American bullion. Bullion worth 15 million ducats reached the imperial treasury in his reign, and his interest rates grew from 17 to 48 per cent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> declared default in 1557, a year after his abdication, and had three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereign-default">bankruptcies</a> by the end of the century.</p><p>Landowners with fixed rents suffered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, though not in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and people on fixed incomes, such as hidalgos and lower clergy, grew poorer, as wages lagged behind prices. In the second half of the century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> made Spanish enterprise less competitive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> became the centre of the international economy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> flourished until the Crown's bankruptcies. Prices then stayed near their early 17th-century levels until the later 18th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Price revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The flood of silver passed through Spain into European financial systems, above all through repayments to the Genoese and German bankers who financed Habsburg campaigns, and contributed to the <b>Price Revolution</b>, a sustained sixteenth-century inflation that damaged Spanish agriculture and manufacturing relative to northern European competitors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
